<!-- Why elastic — two cards, each a small chart drawn with path shapes in a <stack>: the demand
     curve (ink), the provisioned capacity (a step line) and the area between them. On the left
     the capacity arrives in big steps and wastes the gap; on the right it hugs the demand. -->
<slide id="elastic" enter="rise">
  <text variant="kicker">01 · Why elastic compute</text>
  <h2>Stop guessing capacity — let it follow demand</h2>
  <grid cols="2" gap="lg" grow="1">
    <shape type="rounded-rect" variant="card" w="fill" h="fill" pad="lg">
      <col gap="sm" h="fill">
        <h3>Traditional provisioning</h3>
        <stack grow="1" basis="0" w="fill">
          <shape type="path" fill="surface" border="none" w="fill" h="fill" d="M0,85 H40 V55 H72 V30 H100 V100 H0 Z"/>
          <shape type="path" fill="none" border="2 solid muted" w="fill" h="fill" d="M0,85 H40 V55 H72 V30 H100"/>
          <shape type="path" fill="none" border="3 solid fg" w="fill" h="fill" d="M0,98 C15,85 25,90 40,78 C50,70 52,58 62,56 C70,55 75,40 85,36 C93,33 97,30 100,22"/>
        </stack>
        <row gap="md">
          <text role="caption"><strong>━</strong> Demand</text>
          <text role="caption" color="muted"><strong>━</strong> Provisioned</text>
          <text role="caption" color="muted"><strong>■</strong> Idle capacity</text>
        </row>
        <p size="20" color="muted">Capacity arrives in big, slow steps. You <strong>pay for idle hardware</strong> most of the time — and <strong>still run short</strong> at peaks.</p>
      </col>
    </shape>
    <shape type="rounded-rect" variant="card" w="fill" h="fill" pad="lg">
      <col gap="sm" h="fill">
        <h3>With Amazon EC2</h3>
        <stack grow="1" basis="0" w="fill">
          <shape type="path" fill="tint(accent1, 80%)" border="none" w="fill" h="fill" d="M0,94 H10 V88 H20 V84 H30 V80 H40 V74 H50 V60 H60 V52 H70 V46 H80 V36 H90 V30 H100 V100 H0 Z"/>
          <shape type="path" fill="none" border="2 solid accent1" w="fill" h="fill" d="M0,94 H10 V88 H20 V84 H30 V80 H40 V74 H50 V60 H60 V52 H70 V46 H80 V36 H90 V30 H100"/>
          <shape type="path" fill="none" border="3 solid fg" w="fill" h="fill" d="M0,98 C15,85 25,90 40,78 C50,70 52,58 62,56 C70,55 75,40 85,36 C93,33 97,30 100,22"/>
        </stack>
        <row gap="md">
          <text role="caption"><strong>━</strong> Demand</text>
          <text role="caption" color="accent1"><strong>━</strong> EC2 capacity</text>
          <text role="caption" color="accent1"><strong>■</strong> Small headroom</text>
        </row>
        <p size="20" color="muted">Add or remove instances in minutes, automatically. <strong>Pay per second</strong> for what you run — <strong>no upfront hardware</strong>.</p>
      </col>
    </shape>
  </grid>
  <note>On the left, the classic data-center pattern: you buy for the peak you expect, sit on idle capacity, and still miss the peak you didn't expect. On the right, Auto Scaling keeps a thin safety margin above demand.</note>
</slide>
